## 2. Modeling Sequence (SolidWorks)

General principle: **build every rotating/reciprocating part on a shared, predictable set of reference planes and axes** so your mates in the assembly are unambiguous later. Establish a global convention now: **Front Plane = the plane containing the cylinder axis and crank axis** (i.e., the plane the piston reciprocates in), **Y-axis = cylinder axis / piston travel direction**.

### 2.1 Piston
1. New part, sketch on **Top Plane** (or Front, pick one and stay consistent): circle Ø39.9 mm → **Extrude** to 35 mm (this is your skirt/crown blank).
2. On the **Front Plane**, sketch the wrist pin bore centerline as a construction line 20 mm down from the top face; **Extruded Cut** Ø10 mm through both sides (creates the pin bore through the bosses).
3. Add pin **bosses**: sketch two rectangles/circles on the outside of the skirt at the pin bore location if you want raised boss pads, **Extrude Boss** ~2 mm proud of the skirt if desired (optional realism detail).
4. Cut **ring grooves**: sketch a rectangle profile on a plane through the cylinder axis near the top of the piston, use **Revolved Cut** around the piston's central axis — repeat for each groove.
5. **Fillet** the crown edge (top edge of piston) — small fillet, ~0.5 mm, for realism and stress relief.
6. **Chamfer** the skirt bottom edge, 1 × 45°, to ease assembly into the liner.
7. Add a **cosmetic thread or oil hole detail** if you want extra polish (optional).
8. **GD&T**: apply a **cylindricity** callout to the skirt OD (this is the classic GD&T control for a piston — it controls the true cylindrical form better than a simple diameter tolerance would), and a **concentricity** (or position, referenced to a datum axis through the skirt) callout on the pin bore relative to the skirt's own axis (Datum A = skirt OD axis).

### 2.2 Connecting Rod
1. Sketch the rod's **center-to-center layout** first on the Front Plane: two circles (Ø18 small end, Ø32 big end) 90 mm apart, connected by the I-beam shank profile lines. This sketch is your "skeleton" — keep it as a separate reference sketch you can hide later.
2. **Extrude** the small end and big end bosses as circular extrudes (12 mm and 16 mm wide respectively) from a sketch on the Front Plane.
3. Model the **I-beam shank**: create a sketch on a plane perpendicular to the rod's long axis (use a **reference plane** normal to the centerline sketch at the shank's midpoint), draw the I-beam cross-section (two flanges + web), then use **Swept Boss/Base** along the centerline sketch (the straight line between small and big end centers) to generate the tapering shank in one continuous sweep. Alternatively, use **Loft** between a small-end-adjacent profile and a big-end-adjacent profile if you want a smoothly tapering I-beam.
4. **Bore the small end and big end**: Extruded Cut Ø10 mm through the small end, Ø20 mm through the big end.
5. Split the big end into **rod + cap**: sketch a split line through the big end bore centerline, use **Split feature** (Insert > Features > Split) to separate the part into two bodies, then save the cap as its own part (Insert > Features > Save Bodies, or just remodel the cap as a separate simple part mating to the same bore — many portfolio builds just model the cap as a standalone part with the same big-end radius for simplicity).
6. Add **bolt holes** through the cap and into the rod: Ø4.2 mm clearance holes on the cap, and Ø3.3 mm tapped-hole call outs (or clearance if you're using nuts) on the rod side, 22 mm apart, symmetric about the big end axis. Use a **linear pattern or mirror** about the bore centerline plane to keep them symmetric.
7. **Fillet** all internal corners of the I-beam (stress concentration relief — good talking point for your FEA).
8. **GD&T**: 
   - **Position tolerance** on the two cap bolt holes, referenced to Datum A (big end bore axis) and Datum B (rod's long-axis plane) — this is a textbook position callout.
   - **Concentricity** (or **position** referenced to a datum axis) between small end and big end bores — critical because misalignment here creates bending in the pin, exactly the kind of thing a reviewer will expect you to know to control.
   - **Parallelism** between the small end and big end bore axes (rod "twist" control) — a nice bonus callout.

### 2.3 Crankshaft
1. Sketch the **main journal** as a circle Ø20 mm on the Front Plane, **Extrude** 25 mm — this is main journal #1.
2. On a plane offset from the end of main journal #1 (offset = web thickness, 8 mm), sketch the crank **web** as a rounded rectangle/ellipse profile sized to reach from the main journal's center out past the crank pin's center, **Extrude** 8 mm.
3. On a plane offset again (through the web), sketch the **crank pin** circle Ø20 mm — note this circle is **not concentric with the main journal**, it's offset by 22.5 mm (the crank throw). Best practice: use a **construction sketch on the end face** with two centerpoints 22.5 mm apart (main axis and pin axis) so every subsequent feature references that one dimension — if you change stroke later, you only edit one sketch.
4. **Extrude** the crank pin 18 mm from the web.
5. Repeat the **web** extrude on the far side of the crank pin (second web, 8 mm), then a second **main journal** extrude (25 mm, back on the original main axis).
6. Add the **counterweight**: sketch on the same offset plane as a web, but shaped as a partial disc/pie-slice extending radially in the direction opposite the crank pin (180° from the pin's offset direction). **Extrude** 8 mm, merged with the web body.
7. Cut the **keyway**: sketch a rectangle 3 mm × 1.8 mm on one main journal's end face, **Extruded Cut** 10 mm deep into the journal.
8. **Fillet** every step/shoulder where journal meets web and web meets pin (real crankshafts live or die on fillet radii — this is a great FEA/stress talking point). Use ~1–2 mm fillets minimum.
9. **GD&T**:
   - **Concentricity** (or runout) between the two main journals, both referenced to a common Datum axis — this is the single most important GD&T callout on a crankshaft in real practice.
   - **Position** tolerance on the crank pin axis relative to the main journal datum axis (controls the stroke accuracy).
   - **Total runout** on the main journals is a nice advanced callout if you want to show you know the difference between runout and concentricity.

### 2.4 Cylinder Liner
1. Sketch a circle Ø48 mm (OD) on the Front Plane, **Extrude** 70 mm.
2. **Extruded Cut**, Ø40 mm bore, through the full length (or to a depth just short of a closed top deck, if you're modeling one end closed).
3. If modeling a mounting flange: sketch on one end face, **Extrude Boss** the flange ring, then **Extruded Cut** the 4× bolt holes using a **circular pattern** on the bolt circle.
4. **Chamfer** the bore's top edge (piston entry) to ease assembly, 1 × 45°.
5. **GD&T**: **cylindricity** on the bore ID (pairs conceptually with the piston's cylindricity callout — you're controlling the mating cylindrical interface from both sides, which is a strong thing to point out in your README), and **perpendicularity** of the bore axis to the base/mounting face.

### 2.5 Piston Rings
1. Sketch a circle pair (OD 40.6 mm, ID 37 mm) on a plane, **Revolve** 360° minus a small gap (or revolve 358° and leave the end gap as an open feature) around the ring's own centerline.
2. Alternatively: full revolve, then a single **Extruded Cut** slot for the end gap.

### 2.6 Gudgeon Pin
1. Sketch a circle Ø10 mm, **Extrude** 32 mm (or **Revolve** if you want to add the hollow bore in the same sketch).
2. If hollow, sketch the Ø6 mm ID as a second circle in the same **Extrude** operation (extrude the annulus directly), or do a separate **Extruded Cut** afterward.
3. **Chamfer** both ends 0.5 × 45°.
4. Cut the **C-clip grooves**: sketch a thin rectangle profile near each end, **Revolved Cut** 360° around the pin axis.

