## 3. Assembly and Mates

Bring parts in, in this order, and fix the **cylinder liner** first (Insert Component, then right-click → Fix, or mate it to the assembly origin) — everything else references off it.

### 3.1 Piston to Cylinder
- **Concentric mate**: piston skirt OD cylindrical face ↔ cylinder bore cylindrical face.
- **Limit distance mate** (Advanced Mate → Distance, with min/max limits) OR simply leave it unconstrained along the bore axis except for the concentric mate — the **wrist pin/rod/crank chain** is what will actually define piston position once the driver mate is running, so you don't need a hard travel-limit mate for the mechanism to work. (Optional: add a **coincident/width mate** between the piston crown and a reference plane only if you want a manual "set TDC position" step before you attach the rod — many builders do this once during initial assembly, then suppress it once the rod/crank chain is fully mated.)

### 3.2 Piston to Connecting Rod (via wrist pin)
- Insert the **wrist pin** and mate it: **Concentric** (pin OD ↔ piston pin bore) and **Concentric** (pin OD ↔ rod small end bore) — since the pin bore and small-end bore share the same axis once both concentric mates are applied, this alone aligns piston and rod on the pin axis.
- **Width mate** (or two coincident mates on the pin bosses' inner faces vs. the rod small end's side faces) to center the rod between the piston's pin bosses axially.
- Leave **rotation free** about the pin axis — do NOT add a coincident/parallel mate between piston and rod faces; the rod must be free to swing relative to the piston as the crank rotates. This rotational DOF is what allows the rod angularity through the stroke.

### 3.3 Connecting Rod to Crankshaft (via crank pin)
- **Concentric mate**: rod big end bore ↔ crankshaft crank pin OD.
- **Width/coincident mate** to center the rod (and cap) axially on the crank pin.
- Again, leave **rotation free** about the crank pin axis — this is the second rotating joint in the four-bar-equivalent slider-crank chain.

### 3.4 Crankshaft to Fixed Reference
- **Concentric mate**: main journal OD ↔ a fixed bearing bore (either model a simple bearing/bracket part, or mate directly to a hole in your base plate/crankcase stand-in).
- **Coincident/width mate** to locate the crankshaft axially against the bearing/bracket face.
- This joint needs **full rotational freedom** about the main journal axis — this is your **driver** joint.

### 3.5 The Driver Mate
Make the **crankshaft-to-fixed-reference concentric mate** the driver. In practice:
- In the **Motion Study**, you don't "flag" a mate as driver by right-clicking it in the mate list — instead, you add a **Rotary Motor** (see Section 4) whose reference is the crankshaft's main journal cylindrical face or a rotation axis through it. That motor becomes the single input that drives the whole chain: crank rotation → crank pin position → rod big-end position → rod small-end position → piston position along the bore.
- Do **not** put a motor or motion driver on the piston or the rod — only the crankshaft. Everything else's motion is a *result*, not an input; that's the entire point of demonstrating a slider-crank mechanism correctly.
- Sanity check before animating: with all mates applied and nothing over-defined (assembly should show 0 DOF remaining error, or exactly the intended free rotations at the two pin joints and the crank main journal — SolidWorks will report the assembly as fully mated with rotational freedom at those three joints, which is correct and expected for a mechanism, not an error).

