## 4. Motion Study Setup (Motion Study, not Motion Analysis)

1. Open the **Motion Study** tab at the bottom of the SolidWorks window (if not visible: right-click any tab → enable "Motion Study").
2. In the **Motion Study Type** dropdown at the top of the MotionManager, set it to **Motion Analysis** if you want physically-accurate results, or leave the default **Animation**/"Basic Motion" mode if you just want a kinematic playback — for a simple constant-RPM slider-crank with no dynamics/loads, **Basic Motion** mode already correctly captures the geometry-driven piston reciprocation from your mates, which is enough for your GIF.
3. Click the **Motor** icon in the MotionManager toolbar → choose **Rotary Motor**.
4. For **Motor Location**, select the crankshaft's main journal cylindrical face (or an axis through it) as the component/direction reference.
5. Set **Motor Type** to **Constant Speed**, enter your desired RPM (e.g., 60 RPM for a slow, clearly-readable animation, or up to a few hundred RPM if you want a snappier clip — 60–120 RPM reads clearly on a GIF).
6. Set the **Motion Study duration** in the timeline (drag the end-of-timeline key at the bottom) to at least one full crank revolution's worth of time at your chosen RPM (e.g., at 60 RPM, one revolution = 1 second, so set the timeline to 1–2 seconds for one or two full cycles).
7. Click **Calculate** (the "play/calculate" button in the MotionManager toolbar) to compute the motion — SolidWorks will step through and solve the mates frame by frame.
8. **Playback**: use the play button to preview; confirm the piston moves smoothly from TDC to BDC and back with no snapping, jumping, or interference (if it snaps, check for an over-constrained mate or a missed concentric mate).
9. **Export**: File → Save Animation (or the "Save Animation" icon in the MotionManager toolbar) → choose **.avi** or **.gif** as the output format, set resolution (e.g., 800×600 or 1080×1080 for a clean GitHub-embeddable square/rect GIF), set frame rate (15–30 fps is plenty for a mechanism GIF), and render.
10. If exporting AVI, convert to GIF afterward with a tool like ffmpeg (`ffmpeg -i motion.avi -vf "fps=15,scale=600:-1" motion.gif`) to keep file size GitHub-friendly (GIFs embedded in READMEs should ideally stay under ~10 MB).

