## 5. Drawings

For a portfolio, don't drawing-out every single part — pick the parts that best showcase GD&T and manufacturing intent. Recommended set:

| Part | Views | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Piston** | Front (with ring grooves dimensioned), Section view through the pin bore axis, Isometric | Shows internal pin boss/ring groove geometry; good spot for the cylindricity + concentricity FCFs |
| **Connecting Rod** | Front, Section through big end bore, Isometric, Detail view of the I-beam cross-section | The single best part to show off GD&T — position tolerances on cap bolts, concentricity between bores |
| **Crankshaft** | Front (showing crank throw dimension clearly), Section through crank pin, Isometric | Shows the offset/throw geometry and lets you place concentricity/runout FCFs on the main journals |
| **Cylinder Liner** | Front, Full Section (showing bore + wall thickness), Isometric | Simple part, but the section view sells the "liner" concept, and cylindricity + perpendicularity FCFs fit naturally |
| **Assembly drawing** | Front section view at one crank angle (e.g., mid-stroke, to show all parts interacting), Isometric exploded view with a BOM balloon callout table | This is your "hero" drawing — the one a recruiter looks at first |

Optional (skip if time-limited): gudgeon pin and piston ring drawings — low GD&T value, mostly redundant with the piston/rod drawings.

For every detail drawing: include a **title block** with part name/number/material/revision, and place your FCFs referencing clearly labeled datums (Datum A, B, C) called out directly on the same view where the datum feature is visible.

